//! The routines built around a loop.
//!
//! 5593 of them, and **every single one** has a loop in it — which is not a
//! filter that was applied but what the shape turned out to be. They are read
//! by the same walk as [`crate::nested_routines`] and
//! [`crate::deeper_routines`] and recorded in the same shape; what separates
//! them is that the walk had to learn the tail of a do-loop, `} while (...);`,
//! before it could read them at all. Learning that one line is what turned them
//! from unreadable into this table.
//!
//! The loop is usually guarded. Only 1205 of the 5593 have a loop at the outermost
//! level; in the other 4388 it sits inside a branch, so the repetition happens
//! only if some question was answered a particular way. A loop in this program
//! is something the routine decides to enter, not something it always does.
//!
//! The repeated part is also the smaller part. 7240 of the 34897 calls these
//! routines make sit inside a loop; the other 27657 are made once. Most of what a
//! routine here does is setting up and finishing, with the repetition in the
//! middle doing comparatively little.
//!
//! A caution about the two loop kinds. 4727 of them use
//! [`Does::RepeatedlyAtLeastOnce`], against 866 using [`Does::Repeatedly`], but
//! that is a fact about the recovered form as much as about the program: a
//! counted loop the compiler knew would run at least once is printed as a
//! do-loop, whichever way it was written. The count says how the loop was
//! rendered, not how it was typed.
//!
//! And 153 of these are constructors, halves in different steps in every one —
//! the fourth separate population in which that has held without exception.

// The addresses are written without separators so the port-mapping importer can
// match them against the recovered functions.
